var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"Every public name in AirDB, with its docstring.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormLeaf","page":"API Reference","text":"FormLeaf\n\nThe scalar values a form can hold: String, Symbol, Bool, Int64, Float64, ComplexF64 and Nothing. A FormScalar wraps one; a FormContainer holds forms and leaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormLeaf"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormNode","page":"API Reference","text":"FormNode\n\nThe concrete kinds of form, as a Union. The operations that produce a form are typed to return this union rather than the abstract Form, so the compiler keeps a scalar form inline instead of boxing it on every read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormNode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormValue","page":"API Reference","text":"FormValue\n\nWhat a FormContainer holds directly: a Form or a FormLeaf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.AbstractAirExtern","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractAirExtern\n\nThe supertype of a host-level value a form can hold without being JSON itself — a promise, a socket, a configuration handle, a cached value. A form stores one inside a FormExtern, which serializes it through serialize_extern and deserialize_extern; the type must be registered with register_extern before a form holding it can be written or read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.AbstractAirExtern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.Database","page":"API Reference","text":"Database\n\nA configured connection to a PostgreSQL database, plus the machinery AirDB keeps around it: a pool of read connections, a weak cache of live VolatileForms, and a single writer actor that persists committed updates in global commit order.\n\nCreate one with create_database (to initialize a new database) or open_database (to open an existing one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.Database"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.DatabaseConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"DatabaseConfig(; dbname, user, host=\"localhost\", port=5432, password=nothing,\n                 sslmode=\"prefer\", connect_timeout=10, readpoolsize=8,\n                 max_batch=1, failure=:abort, retries=nothing, retry_time=nothing,\n                 retry_delay=0.1, retry_delay_max=5.0, on_error=nothing,\n                 conninfo=nothing)\nDatabaseConfig(url::AbstractString; kwargs...)\n\nThe parameters AirDB uses to reach a PostgreSQL database and to decide how it behaves. The server is the user's to configure and manage; AirDB only connects.\n\ndbname and user are required unless conninfo is given. conninfo, when set, is a libpq connection string or URI used verbatim (as built by the url method), and the structured fields are ignored.\n\nreadpoolsize — how many read connections to keep in the pool.\nmax_batch — how many committed updates the writer actor accumulates before flushing. 1 (the default) writes every commit as soon as it arrives.\nfailure — what to do when a database operation fails:\n:abort (the default) — the writer actor is poisoned and every database operation raises.\n:retry — the failing operation is retried, reconnecting first, while the retry budget lasts. Reads block until a retry succeeds or the budget is exhausted; writes accumulate in the writer's queue meanwhile.\nretries / retry_time — the retry budget: at most this many attempts, or at most this many seconds. If both are given, whichever runs out first wins; if neither is given under :retry, a small default is used.\nretry_delay / retry_delay_max — the delay before the first retry and its ceiling; the delay doubles after each failed attempt up to the ceiling.\non_error — a hook on_error(db, err) called on every database error, before the policy is applied. It may return :retry or :abort to override the policy, an Integer to retry at most that many more times, a Real to retry for that many seconds, or nothing to use the configured policy. It can read pending_writes on db to see the unsaved updates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.DatabaseConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.DatabaseError","page":"API Reference","text":"DatabaseError(cause, failed_writes)\n\nRaised, or recorded on a Database, when a database operation fails under the :abort policy. cause is the underlying exception and failed_writes holds the updates that could not be saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.DatabaseError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.ExternError","page":"API Reference","text":"ExternError\n\nRaised when an extern cannot be written or read: its type is not registered, or a form names an extern type the process has not registered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.ExternError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.ExternTombstone","page":"API Reference","text":"ExternTombstone\n\nRaised when a form is read back and its extern was written as a tombstone — the symbol :abandoned (an active primitive, like an unresolved promise, that could not be persisted) or :expired. The name is the extern's registered type and kind is the tombstone symbol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.ExternTombstone"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.Form","page":"API Reference","text":"Form\n\nThe abstract supertype of every form: FormScalar (a bare leaf), FormContainer (a sequence and a map), and VolatileForm (a form that persists itself). The concrete kinds are gathered in FormNode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.Form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.Form-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Bool, Float64, Int64, String, Symbol, ComplexF64}}","page":"API Reference","text":"Form(x::FormLeaf) -> FormScalar\nForm(seq, map) -> FormContainer\nform(seqargs...; mapargs...) -> FormContainer\n\nForm(x) for a leaf builds a scalar form, and Form(seq, map) builds a container from collections. form(...) is the general builder: its positional arguments become the sequence and its keyword arguments the map, each converted the way to_form does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.Form"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormActorState","page":"API Reference","text":"FormActorState\n\nThe value held by a database's writer Actor. It owns the writer connection, the queues of updates and name registrations not yet written, and the running transaction id. Holding the queues here (rather than writing immediately) is what lets a later policy batch several commits into one database transaction; the strong references in pending keep those forms alive until they are written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormActorState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormContainer","page":"API Reference","text":"FormContainer(seq, map)\n\nA form holding a sequence of positional values (seq) and a map of symbol-keyed values (map), each a FormValue. Build one with form, to_form, or from prepared collections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormContainer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormExtern","page":"API Reference","text":"FormExtern(value)\n\nA form holding a host-level AbstractAirExtern: a value that cannot be written as JSON directly and is therefore stored through a provider's serialize_extern/deserialize_extern. Build one with to_form or FormExtern(x); use value to get the wrapped object back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormExtern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormNotFound","page":"API Reference","text":"FormNotFound(id)\n\nRaised when a form's id is not present in the database.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormNotFound"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.FormScalar","page":"API Reference","text":"FormScalar(value)\n\nA form holding just a leaf: a String, Symbol, Bool, Int64, Float64, ComplexF64 or Nothing. Form(x) for a leaf x builds one, and indexing a container wraps a leaf into one, so every value read out of a form is a form. Use value to get the leaf back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.FormScalar"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.MemoryDatabase-Tuple{}","page":"API Reference","text":"MemoryDatabase() -> MemoryDatabase\n\nA fake, in-memory Database for testing without PostgreSQL. It behaves like a real database — forms persist across commits, named forms work, sync blocks — but keeps everything in memory, saves nothing, and keeps no history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.MemoryDatabase"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AirDB.VolatileForm","page":"API Reference","text":"VolatileForm\n\nA Form that is stored in a PostgreSQL database and kept in sync with it. A VolatileForm is a thin wrapper around an Air Volatile{Form}: it can be read at any time, but it may only be written inside an Air transaction (@tx/tx), and when that transaction commits the new value is sent to the database's writer actor.\n\nEvery VolatileForm has a stable UUID, and forms may embed other volatile forms (written \"@<uuid>\" in JSON). Volatile forms are cached per Database by id, so the same form is always the same object within one process.\n\nCreate one with VolatileForm(db, args...; kwargs...) or VolatileForm(db, form::Form); retrieve an existing one with get_form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.VolatileForm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.VolatileForm-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase, Form}","page":"API Reference","text":"VolatileForm(db::AbstractDatabase, form::Form) -> VolatileForm\nVolatileForm(db::AbstractDatabase, args...; kwargs...) -> VolatileForm\n\nCreates a new volatile form with a fresh UUID and registers it with db so it will be written to the database. The arguments of the second form are the same as Form's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.VolatileForm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Air.setindex-Tuple{Form, Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"setindex(f, v, idxs...) -> FormContainer\n\nA copy of f with the value at the path idxs... replaced by v. Purely functional: no VolatileForm is written. A volatile on the update path is eliminated (replaced by its plain value), so the result never contains a volatile whose content the update would have changed; volatiles off the path are kept as links.\n\nA vector key (or :) fans out and builds a new form over the selection.\n\nAn integer index equal to one past the end of a sequence appends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Air.setindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB._conninfo-Tuple{DatabaseConfig}","page":"API Reference","text":"_conninfo(cfg::DatabaseConfig) -> String\n\nThe libpq connection string for the config: conninfo verbatim when it was set, otherwise built from the structured fields.\n\nThis is part of AirDB's internal implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB._conninfo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.create_database-Tuple{DatabaseConfig}","page":"API Reference","text":"create_database(config::DatabaseConfig; overwrite::Bool=false) -> Database\n\nInitializes a new AirDB database in the PostgreSQL instance described by config — creating the form_history, form_current, and airdb_meta tables and starting a Database for it. The server itself is the user's to provide and manage.\n\nWith overwrite=true the three AirDB tables are dropped and recreated first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.create_database"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.deep_map-Tuple{Any, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"deep_map(fn, f) -> FormNode\n\nfn applied to every node of the tree, bottom-up (a post-order walk): each child is transformed first, then fn is applied to the rebuilt parent. A VolatileForm is passed to fn as the object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.deep_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.deserialize_extern-Union{Tuple{name}, Tuple{Val{name}, Any}} where name","page":"API Reference","text":"deserialize_extern(::Val{name}, payload) -> AbstractAirExtern\n\nRebuild the extern registered under name from the payload the reader parsed. A provider defines a method for each name it registers, dispatching on the Val that carries the name:\n\ndeserialize_extern(::Val{:promise}, payload) = Promise(payload)\n\nThe payload is ordinary Julia data — a leaf, a Vector, a Dict — because an extern is not required to serialize as a form. The default raises.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.deserialize_extern"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.form-Tuple","page":"API Reference","text":"form(seqargs...; mapargs...) -> FormContainer\n\nBuild a container from its arguments: the positional arguments become the sequence and the keyword arguments the map, each converted the way to_form does.\n\nExamples\n\nform(\"widget\", 1, meta = form(name = \"abc\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.from_JSON-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"from_JSON(s::AbstractString) -> Form\nfrom_JSON(resolve::Function, s::AbstractString) -> Form\nfrom_JSON(db::Database, s::AbstractString) -> Form\n\nReads a Form from the JSON that to_JSON writes. A container is a list [seq, map, held] — a two-element [seq, map] is accepted too, with held false; a bare object is accepted as a form with only a map, and a bare scalar as a scalar form, since both are convenient ways to write one by hand.\n\nA form may link to a VolatileForm, written \"@<uuid>\". Resolving such a link requires a database, so either pass one, or pass a function that turns a UUID into the value the link should yield. Reading a form that contains a link without a resolver is an error.\n\nA list beginning with the tag extern is a FormExtern: its type is looked up in the registry (see register_extern) and rebuilt with deserialize_extern. An unregistered type is an ExternError, and a tombstone payload (:abandoned or :expired) is an ExternTombstone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.from_JSON"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.from_JSON-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase, AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"from_JSON(db::AbstractDatabase, s::AbstractString) -> Form\n\nReads a form from JSON, resolving any \"@<uuid>\" links to the (possibly not yet loaded) VolatileForms cached by db.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.from_JSON"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.get_form-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase, Base.UUID}","page":"API Reference","text":"get_form(db::AbstractDatabase, id::UUID) -> VolatileForm\n\nThe volatile form with the given id. If it is already cached it is returned immediately; otherwise an unloaded form is created (its body is read from the database on first access). The same id always yields the same object within a Database.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.get_form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.hold-Tuple{FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"hold(f) -> Form\nunhold(f) -> Form\nisheld(f) -> Bool\n\nhold(f) marks a form as held. A container gets held = true — a copy when it was not already held, f itself when it was — a symbol scalar form gets a leading colon on its name unless it already has one, and any other form (a non-symbol scalar, a volatile) is returned unchanged. unhold undoes it, and isheld tests it.\n\nThe flag is part of a form's value: a held form is not equal to an unheld one, the two hash differently, and it is written in the form's JSON, so a held form round-trips through the database.\n\nExamples\n\nhold(form(a = 1))          # the same form, now held\nhold(Form(:abc))           # Form(:abc) with a leading colon: Form(Symbol(\":abc\"))\nhold(Form(Symbol(\":abc\"))) # unchanged — colons do not stack\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.hold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.isheld-Tuple{FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"isheld(f) -> Bool\n\nWhether f is held — a container with held = true, or a symbol scalar form whose name starts with a colon. Any other form is not held. See hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.isheld"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.isloaded-Tuple{VolatileForm}","page":"API Reference","text":"isloaded(vf::VolatileForm) -> Bool\n\nWhether vf's form body has been read into memory. An unloaded form still knows its id and will load on first access.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.isloaded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.load!-Tuple{VolatileForm}","page":"API Reference","text":"load!(vf::VolatileForm) -> VolatileForm\n\nReads vf's form body from the database if it is not already loaded, and yields vf. Loading only fetches vf itself; the forms it links to stay unloaded until they are touched in turn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.load!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.map_keys-Tuple{Any, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"map_keys(fn, f) -> FormContainer\n\nA form with every map key replaced by fn(key); the sequence is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.map_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.map_values-Tuple{Any, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"map_values(fn, f) -> FormContainer\n\nLike map, but fn receives each value as a form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.map_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.merge_with-Tuple{Any, FormContainer, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"merge_with(fn, f, g) -> FormContainer\n\nLike merge, but a key present in both forms is combined with fn(f[key], g[key]) (each value passed as a form).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.merge_with"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.name_form!-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase, Any, VolatileForm}","page":"API Reference","text":"name_form!(db::Database, name, vf::VolatileForm) -> VolatileForm\n\nRegisters vf under name, overwriting any form already registered under it. Transactional, like named_form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.name_form!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.named_form-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"named_form(db::Database, name; create = false, value = nothing)\n\nLook up the form registered under name, which may be a Symbol or a String. create controls what happens when the name is not found:\n\ncreate = false (the default) — raise if no form is registered under name;\ncreate = true — create a new named form and return it, raising if the name is already in use;\ncreate = nothing — never raise; return nothing when the name is not found.\n\nvalue is the new form's initial value, used only when a form is created. It is converted with to_form, so a Form is used as-is, a number becomes a FormScalar, a collection becomes a container, and leaving it out gives an empty container; or it may be an existing VolatileForm, in which case no new form is made and the name is pointed at the given one.\n\nCreating is transactional: it joins the caller's transaction if there is one, and otherwise runs in a transaction of its own.\n\nExamples\n\nroot = named_form(db, \"root\"; create = true, value = form(version = 1))\nnamed_form(db, \"root\")                 # the same form\nnamed_form(db, :root)                  # Symbol and String agree\nnamed_form(db, \"root\"; create = true)  # ERROR: already in use\nnamed_form(db, \"other\"; create = nothing)   # nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.named_form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.named_forms-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase}","page":"API Reference","text":"named_forms(db::Database) -> PDict{String,VolatileForm}\n\nThe whole name table as it currently stands. The PDict is immutable, so it is safe to share and to keep; each value is a (possibly not-yet-loaded) VolatileForm. Inside a transaction this registers a read of the table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.named_forms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.open_database-Tuple{DatabaseConfig}","page":"API Reference","text":"open_database(config::DatabaseConfig) -> Database\n\nOpens an AirDB database that already exists in the PostgreSQL instance described by config. Raises if the AirDB schema is missing (use create_database to make one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.open_database"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.pending_writes-Tuple{AirDB.AbstractDatabase}","page":"API Reference","text":"pending_writes(db::Database)\n\nThe updates the writer has not yet saved. This is meant for use inside an on_error hook, where the writer is between attempts and its queue is the work that a successful retry would save. If the writer has already failed, the writes from the failed batch are returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.pending_writes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.register_extern-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractAirExtern","page":"API Reference","text":"register_extern(name::Symbol, ::Type{T}; isequal, hash) -> ExternRegistration\n\nRegister the extern type T under name, so that forms holding it can be written and read. name is what appears in the stored JSON — register_extern(:promise, Promise) writes [\"extern\", \":promise\", …] — and is looked up again on read.\n\nisequal and hash are the equality and hash used for FormExterns wrapping a T, and they must agree with one another (equal externs hash equal). Both default to identity of the object, which is the safe choice for a live handle; supply them to compare by value instead:\n\nregister_extern(:config, Config;\n                isequal = (a, b) -> a.version == b.version,\n                hash = (x, h) -> hash(x.version, h))\n\nRegistering the same name and type twice is a no-op; registering a name already taken by a different type is an error.\n\nThe registry lives in memory, so a package that provides an extern type should register it from its __init__, which runs when the package is loaded — a top-level call would run at precompilation and be lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.register_extern"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.select_keys-Tuple{FormContainer, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"select_keys(f, ks) -> FormContainer\n\nA form with only the given keys, in the order given; keys that are not present are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.select_keys"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.serialize_extern-Tuple{AbstractAirExtern}","page":"API Reference","text":"serialize_extern(obj::AbstractAirExtern) -> payload\n\nThe JSON-able payload that stands for obj in the database: a leaf, a Vector, a Dict, or a Form. A provider defines a method for each extern type it owns — importing the function first, since it is AirDB's:\n\nimport AirDB: serialize_extern\nserialize_extern(x::MyExtern) = …\n\nThe default raises, so an extern type that never implemented serialization fails loudly rather than being stored as a hole.\n\nserialize_extern runs on the database's writer thread, while the committed form is encoded, so it must not block. An extern that stands for something that may not be ready should return a tombstone (see ExternTombstone) rather than wait for it:\n\nserialize_extern(p::Promise) = isready(p) ? fetch(p) : :abandoned\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.serialize_extern"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.status-Tuple{Database}","page":"API Reference","text":"status(db::Database) -> (npending, error)\n\nThe number of updates queued for the writer but not yet written, and the last error the writer saw (or nothing). If the writer actor is poisoned, the error is returned as the second element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.status"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.sync-Tuple{Database}","page":"API Reference","text":"sync(db::Database)\n\nBlocks until every update sent to db before this call has been written to the database. The writer actor is sequential, so once it has processed the sync message every earlier write is durable.\n\nRaises the recorded DatabaseError if the flush failed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.sync"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.to_JSON-Tuple{Form}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_JSON(f::Form) -> String\n\nThe form as a JSON string. A container is written as the three-element list [seq, map, held], so the format is self-describing: from_JSON reads a list as a container and nothing else. A scalar form is written as its bare leaf.\n\nA String and a Symbol are both written as JSON strings, with the first character inside the quotes saying which: ' for a string and : for a symbol. A Float64 is a plain number, always with a decimal point or an exponent so that it reads back as a float rather than as an integer. A complex number has no JSON equivalent and is written as {\"re\": …, \"im\": …}, always, even when its imaginary part is zero. A VolatileForm is written as the string \"@<uuid>\"; it is never expanded, so cyclic links are safe to write. A FormExtern is written as the tagged list [\"extern\", \":<type>\", <payload>], where the payload is whatever serialize_extern returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.to_JSON"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.to_JSON-Tuple{VolatileForm}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_JSON(vf::VolatileForm) -> String\n\nThe volatile form's link text, \"@<uuid>\" (without the surrounding JSON quotes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.to_JSON"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.to_dict-Tuple{FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_dict(f) -> Dict{Symbol,Any}\n\nThe map of f as a Dict of ordinary Julia values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.to_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.to_form-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_form(x) -> Form\n\nx as a form: a form is returned as it is, a leaf becomes a FormScalar, and a Vector/PVector or Dict/PDict becomes a FormContainer. Anything else raises.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.to_form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.to_vector-Tuple{FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_vector(f) -> Vector\n\nThe sequence of f as a Vector of ordinary Julia values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.to_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.unhold-Tuple{FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"unhold(f) -> Form\n\nUndoes hold: clears a container's held flag, or strips a symbol scalar form's leading colon. Any other form is returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.unhold"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.update!-Tuple{Union{FormContainer, VolatileForm, FormExtern, FormScalar}, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"update!(f, fn, path...) -> FormContainer\n\nLike update, but transactional: it writes the deepest VolatileForm on the path, exactly as setindex! does, and raises if there is none. Must run inside a transaction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.update-Tuple{Union{FormContainer, VolatileForm, FormExtern, FormScalar}, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"update(f, fn, path...) -> FormContainer\n\nsetindex(f, fn(f[path...]), path...): read the value at path..., apply fn, and write the result back. Purely functional.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.update"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#AirDB.value-Tuple{FormScalar}","page":"API Reference","text":"value(fs::FormScalar)\nvalue(fe::FormExtern)\n\nThe leaf a scalar form wraps, or the extern object an extern form wraps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AirDB.value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.close-Tuple{Database}","page":"API Reference","text":"close(db::Database)\n\nFlushes any queued writes and closes every connection the database holds. Further operations raise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.close"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.filter-Tuple{Any, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"filter(pred, f) -> FormNode\n\nThe form with only the entries whose value satisfies pred (each value passed as a form). A scalar form is kept or dropped as a whole.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.filter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.get-Tuple{Form, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"get(f, idxs..., default) -> Form or default\n\nLike getindex, but a missing final key yields default instead of raising. A missing intermediate key still raises.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.get"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{Union{FormContainer, VolatileForm, FormExtern, FormScalar}, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"f[idxs...] -> Form\n\nIndex into a form along a path of keys. An Integer is a sequence position, a Symbol a map key, and a VolatileForm met along the way is transparent (its value is used). The value at the final key is returned as a form — a leaf comes back as a FormScalar (use value to get the leaf), and a final VolatileForm is returned rather than expanded.\n\nA vector key (or :, meaning every sequence position and map key) fans out: the result is a new FormContainer with one entry per key, built by the outer product of the vector keys, with volatiles eliminated. Missing keys raise.\n\nExamples\n\nf = form(10, meta = form(n = 1))\nf[1]              # Form(10)\nvalue(f[1])       # 10\nf[:meta, :n]      # Form(1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{VolatileForm}","page":"API Reference","text":"vf[]\n\nThe volatile form's current Form. Reads the body from the database on first use. Inside a transaction this registers an Air read, so the transaction sees a consistent value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.haskey-Tuple{Union{FormContainer, VolatileForm, FormExtern, FormScalar}, Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"haskey(f, path...) -> Bool\n\nWhether the whole path resolves. A missing intermediate or final key gives false (rather than raising), and a VolatileForm on the path is transparent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.haskey"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.map-Tuple{Any, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"map(fn, f) -> Form\n\nA form with the same keys as f (both its sequence and its map), every immediate value replaced by fn(value). fn receives a VolatileForm value as the VolatileForm object, so it decides whether to keep or drop it. The results are converted the way any form value is.\n\nIf f is a VolatileForm, the function is applied to its value; if f is a FormScalar, it is applied to the leaf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.merge-Tuple{FormContainer, FormContainer}","page":"API Reference","text":"merge(f, g, ...) -> FormContainer\n\nCombines forms: the sequences are concatenated and the maps are merged, a later form winning on a shared key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.setindex!-Tuple{Form, Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"setindex!(f, v, idxs...) -> v\n\nWalks the path idxs... and sets the value of the deepest VolatileForm on it — the last volatile among the values along the path, including the final one. The volatile is rebuilt along the keys below it. If no value on the path is a volatile, the call raises, because a persistent Form cannot be mutated.\n\nBecause it writes a volatile, this must run inside an Air transaction (@tx/tx); the outer volatiles are read (and registered with the transaction), and only the target is written.\n\nAn integer index equal to one past the end of a sequence appends.\n\nExamples\n\na = VolatileForm(db, n = 0)\n@tx a[:n] = 5            # writes the volatile a\n@tx a[:deep, :n] = 1     # writes the deepest volatile on the path\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"section","location":"#AirDB.jl","page":"Home","text":"A PostgreSQL-backed persistence layer built on Air.jl's software transactional memory.\n\nAirDB lets you keep shared application state in memory as ordinary Julia objects and have every committed change written to PostgreSQL automatically, in transaction order, without locks in your own code. It is aimed at asynchronous, multithreaded servers whose threads share data and whose write order matters.\n\nusing AirDB\nusing Air                  # for @tx / tx\n\ncfg = DatabaseConfig(; host = \"localhost\", dbname = \"airdb\", user = \"airdb\")\ndb = create_database(cfg; overwrite = true)\n\nwidget = VolatileForm(db, \"widget\", n = 1)   # a form, persisted on commit\n@tx widget[:n] = 42                          # change it inside a transaction\nAirDB.sync(db)                               # wait until the writer has saved it\nvalue(widget[:n])                            # 42\n\nThe pieces:\n\nForm — an immutable, JSON-able nested value. A form is a FormScalar (a bare leaf), a FormContainer (a sequence and a map), a VolatileForm, or a FormExtern (a host-level value that is not JSON itself).\nVolatileForm — a Form with a stable UUID that persists itself when the transaction that changed it commits, loaded lazily and cached by id.\nAbstractAirExtern — a host-level value a form can hold (a promise, a socket, a config handle), stored through serialize_extern/deserialize_extern and registered with register_extern.\nDatabase — the connection, the single writer actor, the read pool, and the cache. create_database makes a new one; open_database opens an existing one; MemoryDatabase is a fake, in-memory one for tests.","title":"AirDB.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-written-guide","page":"Home","text":"A narrative guide — getting started, forms, extern types, transactions, indexing, persistence, concurrency, and failures, with worked examples — is in the book.","title":"The written guide"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"See the API Reference for the exported names, or ?name at the REPL for any docstring.","title":"API reference"}]
}
